From 3a118cfa8304cc69e19417f81ce02abb895362b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lajosh=C3=A1zi=2C=20L=C3=A1szl=C3=B3=20Gergely?=
 <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 05:03:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] camera: let every camera setting be chosen by hand

Automatic exposure, gain and white balance are right most of the time and
wrong exactly when a picture is worth taking by hand: a scene that is meant
to be dark, one that is meant to be warm, a shutter speed chosen to freeze
something. Add a group in the preferences where each of them can be taken
over, alongside the autofocus switch that was already there.

Nothing in it is written per control. PipeWire's node describes what the
camera offers - the libcamera name, the type, the bounds, and for the choices
their labels - so the rows are built from what pw-dump reports rather than
from a list in the source: a switch for the controls that decide who chooses
(ExposureTimeMode, AnalogueGainMode, AwbEnable, AfMode), the value they govern
revealed underneath, and a plain number for anything else the camera exposes,
such as contrast and gamma. A camera offering a control this app has never
heard of still gets a working row, under the name the camera gave it.

A control the camera does not offer produces no row at all, which is how the
focus distance behaves: libcamera only publishes LensPosition once the lens
has been related to real distances, so the switch for it appears when that
calibration does, and not before.

Choices are remembered per control name and put back when the camera next
becomes ready, so the camera starts where it was left.
